<h2 class="g-h2" id="behaviour">Behaviour</h2>
<div class="g-prose">
  <p>The tab is in the URL hash. Changing tab adds a history entry, so Back returns to the tab you were on; moving within a tab replaces it. A hash can name a tab, a section, an Authorities or citing row, a footnote, or a quoted passage (<code>#q=…</code>), and the page opens there. The right panel follows the tab: the citator's summary on Opinion, Narrow by on the lists, the signal key on History.</p>
  <p>The thin document rail appears on Opinion only; the other tabs keep their list or history navigation. <a href="/navigation/document-rail/#search">Find</a> widens the existing search control and reveals matching excerpts below it. Keyboard tab changes use a neutral focus background and bottom underline; pointer activation does not leave a focus rectangle. The reader keeps a balanced inner gutter beside the right panel.</p>
  <p>Activating a cited case opens the bottom occurrence panel. It steps through the actual mentions; Open case is a separate action. Available treatment records provide analysis, while plain opinion links report when that analysis is unavailable. See <a href="/navigation/opinion-text/">Opinion text</a> for the markup and input behavior.</p>
  <p>The panel's <em>Why?</em> card names the treatment that sets the page's signal, its court and passage, with a link to its row.</p>
</div>

